Fillets: the corner where a rounding ends (OnDiff) when the face at the end
is split in two faces of one plane, each holding one of the arcs the
rounding's ends lie on (mitcad#239). Example: a pad on a side wall, its
foot a face of its own beside the bottom face; the concave edge between
the wall and the pad's bevelled end is rounded wider than the bevel, so
the ball rolls on the pad's top edge, and the rounding ends on the wall's
bottom edge (bottom face) and at the pad's bottom vertex (the pad's foot
face). No face held both arcs, the corner went to the intersection at the
end, which built a curve on the face at the end from the wrong edge's
geometry, and the topological build gave unorientable faces.

- ChFi3d_CoplanarSplitEndFace (PerformOneCorner, OnDiff, after FindFace
  and ChFi3d_ObstacleEndOnFace fail): where exactly one of the two arcs
  holds the corner's vertex, the faces of the two arcs beside the blend's
  own faces are planes of one plane facing the same way and both hold the
  vertex (an end point at a vertex is first given another edge at that
  vertex bounding the face its side touches and the coplanar face), the
  cap goes on the face of the arc without the vertex, and the part of the
  other arc between its end point and the vertex, cut off that arc as
  under the blend, becomes an edge between the two faces. The cap's
  orientation is taken from the arc on the face it goes on. Elsewhere
  nothing changes.
